      "text": "To find the total surface area of the right pyramid, we need to calculate the area of the square base and the areas of the four triangular faces.\n\n1. **Area of the Square Base:**\n   The base is a square with side length \\( s = 10 \\) cm.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of the base} = s^2 = 10^2 = 100 \\text{ cm}^2\n   \\]\n\n2. **Area of One Triangular Face:**\n   Each triangular face is an isosceles triangle with a base of 10 cm and a height (slant height) that we need to find. The slant height can be found using the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle formed by the slant height, half the base, and the height from the peak to the center of the base.\n   The distance from the center of the base to the midpoint of one side is half the side length of the square:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{s}{2} = \\frac{10}{2} = 5 \\text{ cm}\n   \\]\n   The height of the pyramid is 12 cm, so the slant height \\( l \\) is:\n   \\[\n   l = \\sqrt{12^2 + 5^2} = \\sqrt{144 + 25} = \\sqrt{169} = 13 \\text{ cm}\n   \\]\n   The area of one triangular face is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of one triangular face} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{slant height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 13 = 65 \\text{ cm}^2\n   \\]\n\n3. **Total Surface Area:**\n   The total surface area is the sum of the area of the base and the areas of the four triangular faces:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total surface area} = \\text{Area of the base} + 4 \\times \\text{Area of one triangular face} = 100 + 4 \\times 65 = 100 + 260 = 360 \\text{ cm}^2\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the total surface area of the pyramid is \\(\\boxed{360}\\) square centimeters.",
